quick Bash scripts that were super helpful during log monitoring and debugging 
These lightweight scripts are handy when:
✅ You want to find the most repeated lines in logs (like frequent errors or alerts)
✅ You’re checking word frequency — spotting repeated terms like error, timeout, failed
✅ You need a fast way to analyze logs using only shell commands (no extra tools needed!)
Below are two simple scripts I wrote — easy to tweak, easy to plug into your daily workflow. 

💡 Script 1: Count Word Frequency in a Log File
This script helps extract and count how often each word appears — useful for identifying common error terms, repeated messages, or even keyword trends in logs.

✅ wordcount.sh

#!/bin/bash

# Check if a filename is passed
if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then
  echo "Usage: sh wordcount.sh <filename>"
  exit 1
fi

FILE="$1"

# Check if file exists
if [ ! -f "$FILE" ]; then
  echo "Error: File '$FILE' not found."
  exit 1
fi

# Count and display word frequencies
tr -s '[:space:]' '\n' < "$FILE" |   # Replace whitespace with newlines (1 word per line)
tr -d '[:punct:]' |                        # Remove punctuation
tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' |             # Convert to lowercase
grep -v '^$' |                            # Remove empty lines
sort | uniq -c | sort -nr              # Count and sort by frequency


Example:
sh wordcount.sh /var/log/app.log


💡 Script 2: Show Top 5 Most Frequent Log Lines
Perfect when you want to identify repeated log lines — like recurring health checks, warnings, or the same error popping up again and again.

✅ top5.sh

#!/bin/bash

# Check if a file is provided
if [ $# -lt 1 ]; then
  echo "Usage: sh top5.sh <filename>"
  exit 1
fi

FILE="$1"

# Check if file exists and is readable
if [ ! -f "$FILE" ]; then
  echo "Error: File '$FILE' not found."
  exit 1
fi

# Display the top 5 most frequent lines
echo "Top 5 most frequent lines in $FILE:"
sort "$FILE" | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 5

 Example:
sh top5.sh /var/log/nginx/access.log
